# "Theory first" primers for the QM exercise sheets: a short, intuitive first introduction to what a sheet question needs,
# for when the lecture was missed. Written like a good script page, not like a summary card: the question first, one picture,
# the few formulas with their meaning, a tiny example, what the sheet question uses, and where to read in the script
# (QM25 script, Gaberdiel; section, pages and equation numbers). Keyed by (sheet, question).
# Style rule: no ASCII apostrophes inside r'...' strings (use ’ in text, ^\prime in math).

def P(question, picture, pieces, example, sheet, read):
    sec = lambda t, b: f'<h4>{t}</h4>{b}'
    return (sec('The question in plain words', question) + sec('The picture', picture) + sec('The pieces you need', pieces)
            + sec('Tiny example', example) + sec('What this sheet question needs', sheet) + sec('Read in the script', read))

PRIMERS = {}

PRIMERS[(9, 1)] = P(
 r'<p>For most potentials the Schrödinger equation is a second-order differential equation you cannot solve by hand. But for a few special potentials there is a shortcut that gives you the ground state from a <b>first-order</b> equation, which you can always integrate. It is the same trick that makes the "elegant solution" of the harmonic oscillator work. This question shows that the trick is not special to the oscillator.</p>',
 r'<p>Think of factorising a number: if you can write $H$ (up to a constant) as $A^\dagger A$, then $H$ can never go below that constant, because $A^\dagger A$ is "a square": $\langle\psi|A^\dagger A|\psi\rangle=\|A\psi\|^2\ge0$. The lowest state is the one that $A$ squashes to zero. So instead of solving $H\psi=E\psi$ you solve $A\psi_0=0$.</p>',
 r'<ul><li>Oscillator (script 6.14–6.18): $a=\frac1{\sqrt2}(x+\partial_x)$, $H=\hbar\omega(a^\dagger a+\frac12)$. Meaning: the energy is $\frac12\hbar\omega$ plus a non-negative part.</li><li>Positivity (6.25): $\langle n|a^\dagger a|n\rangle=\|a|n\rangle\|^2=n\ge0$. Meaning: the spectrum is bounded from below.</li><li>Ground state (6.28): $a|0\rangle=0$, in position space $(x+\partial_x)\psi_0=0$, a first-order equation: $\psi_0\propto e^{-x^2/2}$.</li><li>General version: with $A=\partial_x+W(x)$ one finds $A^\dagger A=-\partial_x^2+W^2-W^\prime$. If your potential (in suitable units) is $W^2-W^\prime$ plus a constant, you win.</li></ul>',
 r'<p>Take $W=x$: $A=\partial_x+x=\sqrt2\,a$ and $A^\dagger A=-\partial_x^2+x^2-1$. That is $\frac{2}{\hbar\omega}H-1$ for the oscillator, so $E_0=\frac12\hbar\omega$, and $A\psi_0=0$ gives $\psi_0^\prime=-x\psi_0$, the Gaussian. Two lines instead of a power series.</p>',
 r'<p>The sheet gives you $W=\tanh x$. Check $W^2-W^\prime=\tanh^2x-\mathrm{sech}^2x=1-2\,\mathrm{sech}^2x$: exactly $\frac{2m}{\hbar^2}V+1$. So $A^\dagger A\psi=(\epsilon+1)\psi$, positivity gives $\epsilon\ge-1$, and $A\psi_0=0$ means $\psi_0^\prime=-\tanh x\,\psi_0$, i.e. $\psi_0\propto1/\cosh x$. For the sketch (part i): an even, node-free bump sitting in the well.</p>',
 r'<p>§6.1.2 "Die elegante Lösung", pp. 61–63, eqs. (6.14)–(6.18), the positivity argument around (6.25)–(6.28), and the ground state (6.31). The factorisation for other potentials is not in the script: it is the point of this exercise.</p>')

PRIMERS[(9, 2)] = P(
 r'<p>Measuring the energy can give either isolated values (an electron bound in an atom) or a whole continuum (a free electron). The question: can you tell which, <b>without solving</b> the Schrödinger equation, just by looking at the potential? Yes: if the particle is confined everywhere, the spectrum is discrete. This question makes that intuition precise.</p>',
 r'<p>Semiclassically, every quantum state occupies a cell of size $2\pi\hbar$ in phase space $(q,p)$ (per dimension). If the classically allowed region below any energy $E$ has <b>finite</b> phase-space area, it contains only finitely many cells: finitely many states below $E$, a discrete spectrum. A free particle can sit anywhere: infinite area, infinitely many states at every energy: a continuum. The "thermometer" that counts the states is the partition function $Z(\beta)=\mathrm{tr}\,e^{-\beta H}$.</p>',
 r'<ul><li>Spectrum (4.37–4.41): the possible measurement results. Free particle $\sigma(H)=\mathbb R_+$ (continuous), finite well $\{E_1,\dots,E_N\}\cup\mathbb R_+$ (mixed), oscillator $\{\hbar\omega(n+\frac12)\}$ (discrete).</li><li>Spectral theorem (4.42): $f(H)=\sum_Ef(E)P_E$. With $f=e^{-\beta E}$: $Z(\beta)=\sum_n e^{-\beta E_n}$ (with degeneracy). A finite answer needs countably many, well-separated levels.</li><li>Golden-Thompson: $\mathrm{tr}\,e^{A+B}\le\mathrm{tr}(e^Ae^B)$. It lets you split kinetic and potential energy as if they commuted, with an inequality instead of an equality.</li><li>Outcome: $Z_{\rm quantum}\le Z_{\rm classical}=\frac1{(2\pi\hbar)^d}\int d^dq\,d^dp\,e^{-\beta H(q,p)}$.</li></ul>',
 r'<p>Oscillator: $Z_{\rm q}=\sum_ne^{-\beta\hbar\omega(n+\frac12)}=\frac{1}{2\sinh(\beta\hbar\omega/2)}$, $Z_{\rm cl}=\frac{1}{2\pi\hbar}\sqrt{\frac{2\pi m}{\beta}}\sqrt{\frac{2\pi}{\beta m\omega^2}}=\frac1{\beta\hbar\omega}$. Both finite, and indeed $\frac1{2\sinh(x/2)}\le\frac1x$. Free particle: the $q$ integral is $\int dq=\infty$: no discreteness.</p>',
 r'<p>(i) Argue "finite trace means discrete" (a continuum makes the trace infinite, e.g. $\langle p|p\rangle=\delta(0)$). (ii) Apply Golden-Thompson to $e^{-\beta(T+V)}$, take the trace in position states, insert momentum states for $e^{-\beta T}$: you get exactly the classical integral. (iii) The $p$-integral is always a Gaussian, so the criterion is $\int d^dq\,e^{-\beta V(q)}<\infty$; for $V=\nu\|q\|^\alpha$ substitute $t=\beta\nu r^\alpha$ to get a Gamma function.</p>',
 r'<p>§4.6 "Verallgemeinerung auf ∞-dimensionale Hilberträume", pp. 48–49: definition of the spectrum (4.37), the three examples (4.39–4.41) and the spectral theorem (4.42–4.47). The partition function and Golden-Thompson are not in the script; they are introduced by this exercise.</p>')

PRIMERS[(9, 3)] = P(
 r'<p>A spin-$\frac12$ state is a vector in $\mathbb C^2$. If you rotate your lab, the spin state must change by some $2\times2$ unitary matrix. Which one rotates a spin by the angle $\omega$ about the axis $\vec n$? The answer is $U=e^{-i\frac\omega2\vec n\cdot\vec\sigma}$, with a surprising half angle that makes a full turn give $-1$.</p>',
 r'<p>The script’s trick: turn a vector into a matrix, $\tilde x=\vec x\cdot\vec\sigma=\begin{pmatrix}x_3&x_1-ix_2\\x_1+ix_2&-x_3\end{pmatrix}$ (7.71). It is hermitian and $\det\tilde x=-|\vec x|^2$ (7.73). Conjugating, $\tilde x\to U\tilde xU^\dagger$ keeps it hermitian and keeps the determinant: the length of $\vec x$ is preserved, so it is a rotation. Every SU(2) matrix gives a rotation, and $U$ and $-U$ give the same one: $SU(2)/\{\pm1\}\simeq SO(3)$ (7.76).</p>',
 r'<ul><li>Generators in the spin-$\frac12$ representation (7.85): $M_j=\sigma_j/2$, and rotations are exponentials of generators, like $R(\vec e_3,\varphi)=e^{-iM_3\varphi}$ (7.77).</li><li>Product rule: $\sigma_j\sigma_k=\delta_{jk}I+i\varepsilon_{jkl}\sigma_l$. Consequence: $(\vec n\cdot\vec\sigma)^2=I$ for a unit vector.</li><li>Because $(\vec n\cdot\vec\sigma)^2=I$, the exponential behaves like $e^{i\theta}=\cos\theta+i\sin\theta$: $U=\cos\frac\omega2\,I-i\sin\frac\omega2\,\vec n\cdot\vec\sigma$.</li><li>Full turn: $U(2\pi)=-I$ (7.78): half-integer spins pick up a sign; only $\pm$ pairs map to one rotation.</li></ul>',
 r'<p>$\vec n=\vec e_3$: $U=\mathrm{diag}(e^{-i\omega/2},e^{i\omega/2})$. Then $U\sigma_1U^\dagger=\cos\omega\,\sigma_1+\sin\omega\,\sigma_2$: the vector $\vec e_1$ went to $\cos\omega\,\vec e_1+\sin\omega\,\vec e_2$, a rotation by $\omega$, although $U$ only contains $\omega/2$.</p>',
 r'<p>(i) Multiply out the Pauli matrices (do $\sigma_1\sigma_2$ and $\sigma_1^2$, the rest is cyclic). (ii) Split the exponential series into even and odd powers using $(\vec n\cdot\vec\sigma)^2=I$. (iii) Compute $U\tilde xU^\dagger$: either for $\vec n=\vec e_3$ and argue by symmetry, or in general to get Rodrigues’ rotation formula.</p>',
 r'<p>§7.5 "SO(3) vs. SU(2)", pp. 75–77, eqs. (7.71)–(7.78); §7.7 "Der Spin des Elektrons", pp. 78–79, eq. (7.85) for $M_j=\sigma_j/2$. §7.6 (projective representations, p. 77) explains why the sign $-1$ is physically allowed.</p>')

PRIMERS[(9, 4)] = P(
 r'<p>The 3D oscillator level $N$ has $\frac{(N+1)(N+2)}2$ states: 1, 3, 6, 10, ... Rotations alone cannot explain this: an irreducible rotation multiplet $D_l$ has only $2l+1$ states, and one level contains several of them ($l=N,N-2,\dots$). Whenever states share an energy "by accident", look for a bigger symmetry. Here it is su(3).</p>',
 r'<p>Picture the $N$ energy quanta as $N$ identical balls in three boxes labelled $x,y,z$. The energy only counts the balls, not which box they are in. The operator $a_i^\dagger a_j$ takes a ball from box $j$ and puts it into box $i$: it changes the state but not the energy. All these "ball movers" commute with $H$, and together they form a symmetry algebra bigger than rotations.</p>',
 r'<ul><li>The 9 operators $J_{ij}=a_i^\dagger a_j$ obey the same brackets as the $3\times3$ matrix units $E_{ij}$: u(3). The trace part $\sum_iJ_{ii}=N$ just counts; the rest is su(3).</li><li>Strategy from §7.4 for so(3): find a vector killed by the raising operator ($M_+\psi_j=0$, (7.37)), then get everything by lowering; finite dimension forces $j$ to be half-integer (7.43).</li><li>New for su(3): two commuting "$M_3$"s ($h_1,h_2$), so weights are pairs $(m,n)$, and three raising operators $e_1,e_2,e_3$. A highest weight vector is killed by all three.</li><li>Each raising operator, its lowering partner and their commutator form a copy of su(2). Inside each copy the §7.4 argument applies: that is why the weights must be integers.</li></ul>',
 r'<p>Level $N=1$: the three states $a_1^\dagger|0\rangle,a_2^\dagger|0\rangle,a_3^\dagger|0\rangle$. The ball movers shuffle them like the components of a vector in $\mathbb C^3$: the fundamental representation of su(3). Highest weight: $a_1^\dagger|0\rangle$, weight $(1,0)$. For level $N$: $(a_1^\dagger)^N|0\rangle$, weight $(N,0)$, dimension $\frac{(N+1)(N+2)}2$.</p>',
 r'<p>(i) $[H,a_i^\dagger a_j]=0$ and matching brackets with $E_{ij}$. (ii) Compute $[h_i,\cdot]$ on the $e$’s and $f$’s: the eigenvalues are the roots. (iii) Repeat the §7.4 argument with three raising operators. (iv) Identify the oscillator level $N$ as the representation with highest weight $(N,0)$. Warm-ups: sheet 7 Q3 (counting the states) and sheet 8 Q3 (the same idea with su(2) in 2D).</p>',
 r'<p>§7.4 "Irreduzible Darstellungen von so(3)", pp. 71–73, eqs. (7.32)–(7.51): the highest-weight method you generalise here. §6.1.2, pp. 61–63, for $a,a^\dagger$. su(3) itself is not in the script: this exercise goes beyond the lecture, so it is fine to need this primer.</p>')
